## 5. Three failure classes

- A, the clock: adaptive chunking, {{ex_a_ours}} for one decision against {{ex_a_bar}}; may work on a faster GPU; easiest to avoid: time the method on your device first.
- B, judges (a model that says success, failure or progress): a 4B reward model, zero-shot on ArmnetBench, AUROC {{ex_b_ours}}; episode length alone {{ex_b_len}}.
- C, latents (a world model's vector computed from the camera): ranks the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, bar {{ex_c_bar}}; a baseline with the actions only: {{ex_c_base}}; the commanded joints already hold the arm's state, so the latent must add the objects, and it does not.
- The classes are our proposal, from the author's reading of the {{c_paper_fail}} failed claims; a second reader is needed.
- Order to test a paper on a cheap rig: time it on the device → the cheapest baseline → then the bar.

## 6. Limitations

- Data: short tasks, one task family, one arm; nothing on long horizon.
- One operator ran the arm and labelled the outcomes, not blind.
- Small samples and one training seed in many tests (the n of each test is in the appendix).
- A fail on our rig is not a fail on the authors' hardware.
- Papers chosen from the stack we run (world models, VLAs, failure monitors, chunking, edge latency): the failure rate describes these papers, not the field.
- Our own mistakes are possible: TurboVLA (Section 3).

## 7. Related work

- On the same arm: a real-world VLA benchmark with a taxonomy of failures and recoveries [@2606.08881]; ArmnetBench, a fleet of SO-101 cells evaluating policies at scale [@2607.24481].
- In simulation: vla-eval, VLAs across 14 benchmarks [@2603.13966]; a benchmark of demonstration quality [@2610.18869].
- They rank policies; we test one paper claim against a bar fixed before, on one cheap rig.
- Closest: RoboSeed [@2610.40026], scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions including reproduction evidence and deployment affordability; its authors say the score is not a probability that a result is correct.
- Ours: one yes or no per claim on one rig, with the data behind it.

## 8. Conclusion

- {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, ${{cost}} of cloud GPU → {{c_paper_repro}} hold.
- Several lost to a baseline with no learning: copy-last, the joints, episode length.
- Advice: run the cheapest baseline first, on the same data, with the bar fixed before the test.
- Everything is public and every number can be rebuilt from the repository.

